      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "92 patients with ischemic heart disease (IHD) have been examined. Unlike stable angina pectoris, unstable angina and acute myocardial infarction occur more often against the aggravation of chronic atrophic gastritis with a high degree of H. pylori colonization in the mucous coat of the antral part of the stomach. The development of repeated coronary phenomena within one year in patients with unstable angina and acute myocardial infarction was related to the high degree of H. pylori colonization in the mucous coat of the stomach. Irrespective of the presence of IHD in patients, increased H. pylori colonization in the mucous coat was accompanied by a drop in the antioxidant protection activity against the growth of the blood malonic dialdehyde content."},
